To determine the wavelength of each object, we use the de Broglie equation: 

\[
\lambda = \frac{h}{p} = \frac{h}{m \cdot v}
\]

where \( h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J·s} \), \( m \) is mass, and \( v \) is velocity. The results are converted to centimeters.

---

### (a) 10,000 kg truck at 27.8 m/s
1. Momentum \( p = 10^4 \, \text{kg} \times 27.8 \, \text{m/s} = 2.78 \times 10^5 \, \text{kg·m/s} \)
2. Wavelength \( \lambda = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{2.78 \times 10^5} \approx 2.3835 \times 10^{-39} \, \text{m} = 2.4 \times 10^{-37} \, \text{cm} \)

---

### (b) 50 mg flea at 1 m/s
1. Mass \( m = 50 \, \text{mg} = 50 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{kg} = 5 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{kg} \)
2. Momentum \( p = 5 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{kg} \times 1 \, \text{m/s} = 5 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{kg·m/s} \)
3. Wavelength \( \lambda = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{5 \times 10^{-5}} \approx 1.3252 \times 10^{-29} \, \text{m} = 1.3 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{cm} \)

---

### (c) Water molecule at 500 m/s
1. Molar mass of water \( = 18 \, \text{g/mol} = 0.018 \, \text{kg/mol} \)
2. Number of molecules per mole \( = 6.022 \times 10^{23} \, \text{molecules/mol} \)
3. Mass \( m = \frac{0.018}{6.022 \times 10^{23}} \approx 2.988 \times 10^{-26} \, \text{kg} \)
4. Momentum \( p = 2.988 \times 10^{-26} \, \text{kg} \times 500 \, \text{m/s} = 1.494 \times 10^{-23} \, \text{kg·m/s} \)
5. Wavelength \( \lambda = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{1.494 \times 10^{-23}} \approx 4.435 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{m} = 4.43 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{cm} \)

---

### Final Results
- (a) \( 2.4 \times 10^{-37} \, \text{cm} \)
- (b) \( 1.3 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{cm} \)
- (c) \( 4.43 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{cm} \)

---

**Correct Answer:** **E. 2.4 × 10⁻³⁷ cm, 1.3 × 10⁻²⁷ cm, 4.43 × 10⁻¹¹ cm**

ANSWER: E
